after almost fifty years on the Upper  East Side the Whitney Museum of American  Art has a new address it's moving back  into lower Manhattan it's a huge deal  just in terms of the size that we have  now and the availability of the  collection just so many more people the  new Whitney sits at the end of the High  Line a park that gets nearly five  million visitors a year we're not  worried that it's going to be empty and  you're going to be able to wander  through the galleries alone we worry  more that it's going to be packed and we  have to figure out how you're going to  have a wonderful experience in the  galleries the Whitney wanted to expand  for decades over the last 25 years there  were three plans to add on to its old  home on Madison Avenue what was it about  this plan to you that made it appealing  to you and other members of the board  the director I think Adam Weinberg the  Alice Pratt brown director of the  Whitney is a true visionary  and he made this happen Weinberg had his  work cut out for him about seven years  ago is when we began and was actually a  bit more protracted process than we  would have hoped because just after we  began 2008 came with a financial crisis  not a good time to retire and we had  raised a certain amount of money but we  basically put the project on a semi  pause at that time  the economy started to recover and in  2013 the board's former chairman Leonard  Lauder gave the museum a hundred and  thirty million dollars we wanted a  building that felt large enough that we  could do and expand our programs show  more of our collection have more  education programs accommodate more  visitors but at the same time have a  building in a sense of circulation that  people would not feel lost or  overwhelmed for museum's expansion is a  tricky thing MoMA continues to attract  long lines of visitors years after it  added more space but the American folk  art museum couldn't afford the payments  on its building ultimately it moved to a  space one-sixth its former size but the  Whitney's director is not worried we  have a very good financial model that  should sustain this institution for the  next hundred years part of that model is  finding new ways to make money I hardly  know any museums anymore that don't rent  some aspect of their spaces at some time  it's it's a necessity we don't have you  know ongoing government support or  ongoing income streams you have to earn  income a non-profit art museum defines  success differently Weinberg says it  can't be all about revenues and reviews  it's about who and what the museum has  championed you had to look back 10 years  and say was the Whitney on it 10 years  ago were they there with the right group  of artists the right ideas the right  thoughts
 

